      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-225031",
      ":question/problem": "A factory has two locations, Location A and Location B. The factory produces $37$ different types of containers, and both locations manufacture all types.\n\nThe site manager wants to know if Location A produces more containers than Location B. He decided to sample the number of containers produced at both locations for all $37$ types and compute the difference $d_{i} = x_{i} - y_{i}$ for each type. Here, $x_{i}$ and $y_{i}$ give the number of type $i$ containers produced at locations A and B, respectively.\n\nAfter conducting their sample, he found that the average difference in the number of types produced was $d^(\u2015) = 0.92$, and the variance of the differences was $s^{2} = 0.65^{2}$.\n\nFill in the missing information in the following statements and construct a $99%$ confidence interval for the mean difference $\u03bc$ between the number of containers produced.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.719) = 0.005$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $36$ degrees of freedom.*\n\nThe end-points of the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference of the production rates, rounded to two decimal places, are approximately\n$0.92$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nThe $99%$ confidence interval {{field-3}}.\nThis confidence interval suggests that Location A produces {{field-4}} containers compared to Location B.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."
