      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-251472",
      ":question/problem": "A restaurant has two branches, Branch $1$ and Branch $2$. The restaurant offers its customers $40$ different dishes, and all dishes are offered in both branches.\n\nThe manager wants to know if Branch $1$ sells more dishes than Branch $2$. He decided to sample the number of orders at both branches for all $40$ dishes and compute the difference $d_{i} = x_{i} - y_{i}$ for each dish. Here, $x_{i}$ and $y_{i}$ give the number orders of dish $i$ at branches $1$ and $2$, respectively.\n\nAfter conducting the sample, he found that the average difference in the number of dishes sold was $d^(\u2015) = 0.54$, and the variance of the differences was $s^{2} = 3.2^{2}$.\n\nFill in the missing information in the following statements and construct a $95%$ confidence interval for the mean difference $\u03bc$ between the number of dishes sold.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.023) = 0.025$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $39$ degrees of freedom.*\n\nThe end-points of the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference in the number of dishes sold, rounded to two decimal places, are approximately\n$3.20$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nThe $95%$ confidence interval {{field-3}}.\nThis confidence interval suggests that Branch $1$ sold {{field-4}} dishes compared to Branch $2$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."
